# Tracer: compiler exact multi-layer unary application
#
# Before (Litex verified, but compiler emission stopped):
# forall S, T, U set, a S, b T, g fn(x S) fn(y T) U:
#     g(a)(b) = g(a)(b)
# Former behavior: the compiler rejected every application with more than one
# source layer, even though the verifier retained a separate callable contract
# and argument-membership proof for each layer.
#
# Now (verified and compiled):
forall S, T, U set, a S, b T, g fn(x S) fn(y T) U:
    g(a)(b) = g(a)(b)
# Current behavior: each unary source layer consumes its own verifier-selected
# function contract and argument-membership proof. The intermediate g(a) value
# remains the exact carrier of the second function set. The three-layer case
# below keeps a second independently certified prefix rather than using a
# two-layer special case.

forall S, T, U, V set, a S, b T, c U, g fn(x S) fn(y T) fn(z U) V:
    g(a)(b)(c) = g(a)(b)(c)
#
# Boundary: multiple parameters in one source layer, such as f(a, b), remain
# fail-closed; layers are never flattened into target-language currying.
# Evidence: target/release/litex -compact -strict -runner -f lean/examples/23_MultilayerApplication.lit
# and cargo test --release --test litex_to_lean_compiler_tracers
